\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We develop a complete structure theory for social aggregators that are linear
over the min-plus (tropical) semiring. An \emph{aggregator} on a finite
electorate $\voters$ with $n=|\voters|$ voters is a map
$F:\R^{\voters}\to\R$ sending a profile of real scores to a social score. We
impose three axioms: preservation of coordinatewise minima (tropical
additivity), equivariance under diagonal translation (tropical scalar
multiplication), and normalization $F(\vzero)=0$.

We prove that every such aggregator is a \emph{tropical linear form}
$F(x)=\min_{i\in S}(x_i+\delta_i)$ for a unique nonempty support $S$ and unique
weights $\delta_i\ge 0$ on $S$ with $\min_{i\in S}\delta_i=0$; the support is
the set of \emph{active} voters. The sharper guess that $F$ must be a minimum of
coordinate projections is \emph{false} --- the two-voter rule
$W(x_0,x_1)=\min(x_0,x_1+1)$ obeys all three axioms yet returns $W(1,-1)=0$, a
value submitted by nobody --- and adding \emph{selectivity} (the social score is
always some individual score) forces all weights to vanish and restores the
projection form.

We identify the Arrow threshold: the family of \emph{dependence sets} of a
tropical aggregator is exactly the principal filter generated by its support, and
this family satisfies the ultrafilter dichotomy if and only if the support is a
singleton, if and only if $F$ is a dictatorship. Anonymity forces the opposite
extreme, $F(x)=\min_i x_i$. We quantify the tradeoff exactly: with support of
size $k$, the permutation orbit of the minimal decisive coalition has
$\binom{n}{k}$ members, of which exactly one is decisive, so the anonymity defect
is precisely $1-1/\binom{n}{k}$.

Geometrically, the profile space decomposes into convex polyhedral chambers, and
labelling each profile by its decisive coalition yields a polyhedral complex
whose face lattice is the reversed lattice of nonempty subcoalitions of the
support, whose walls containing a cell labelled $T$ are exactly the edges of the
complete graph on $T$ (the flat of the graphic matroid of $K_S$ spanned by
$K_T$, with any star of $T$ as a basis), and whose cell of $T$ has codimension
exactly $|T|-1$. We prove a \emph{single-voter exchange law}: changing one
voter's score moves a profile from one chamber across the intervening wall into
another.

Analytically, the log-sum-exp family
$F_\varepsilon(x)=-\varepsilon\log\sum_{i\in S}e^{-(x_i+\delta_i)/\varepsilon}$
satisfies the explicit sandwich
$F(x)-\varepsilon\log|S|\le F_\varepsilon(x)\le F(x)$, hence converges to $F$
uniformly in the profile with rate $\varepsilon\log|S|$; and the deformation is
exact for all $\varepsilon>0$ if and only if the support is a singleton. Thus
dictatorship is precisely the rigid point of Maslov dequantization.
\end{abstract}

